  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive breakdown of the word **INDOLENT**:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd6 Definition\n\n**Indolent** is an adjective meaning **slow or lazy**. More specifically, it means **averse to activity, effort, or movement; habitually lazy**.\n\nAn indolent person is slow and lazy \u2014 not the type of person you'd want running your corporation or competing with you in a relay race.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 Medical Usage\n\nDoctors also use the word **indolent** to describe medical conditions that are **slow to progress**. If you're diagnosed with an illness, you'd prefer an indolent one over one that spreads quickly.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd24 Part of Speech\n- **Adjective** (formal)\n- The noun form is **indolence** (e.g., *\"Some argue that television encourages indolence.\"*)\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf31 Etymology (Word Origin)\n\nThe word comes from **Latin** *indol\u0113ns*, meaning \"not feeling pain,\" from *in-* (not) + *dol\u0113re* (to grieve or cause distress). The main modern sense of \"laziness, love of ease\" emerged around **1710**, perhaps reflecting the notion of avoiding trouble.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdd Synonyms\nSynonyms include: **lazy, slack, idle, slow, sluggish, inactive, inert, languid, lethargic, listless, lackadaisical, torpid, slothful**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcac Example Sentences\n- *\"She was described as kind, passive, and indolent.\"*\n- *\"He was as indolent as ever and showed no very strenuous desire to hunt up an occupation.\"*\n\nIn short, **indolent** describes someone (or something) that avoids effort, is habitually lazy, or \u2014 in a medical context \u2014 progresses slowly.",
